      "ct-distance": {
        "title": "Hamming distance and the Singleton bound",
        "questions": [
          {
            "type": "numeric",
            "q": "A binary code of length $n=15$ has minimum distance $d=5$. How many errors can it guarantee to correct?",
            "answer": 2,
            "tol": 0,
            "explain": "$t=\\lfloor (d-1)/2\\rfloor=\\lfloor 4/2\\rfloor=2$. Up to $d-1=4$ errors are detectable; up to $\\lfloor(d-1)/2\\rfloor=2$ are correctable, because closed Hamming balls of radius $2$ around distinct codewords are disjoint.",
            "hint": "Use $t=\\lfloor (d-1)/2\\rfloor$."
          },
          {
            "type": "mcq",
            "q": "A code with parameters $[n,k,d]=[10,4,d]$ over any alphabet must satisfy which Singleton inequality?",
            "choices": [
              "$d \\le n + k - 1 = 13$",
              "$d \\le n - k + 1 = 7$",
              "$d \\le k = 4$",
              "$d \\le n/k = 2.5$"
            ],
            "answer": 1,
            "explain": "Singleton: $d\\le n-k+1$. Puncturing $d-1$ coordinates of $C$ still leaves $|C|=q^k$ distinct strings of length $n-d+1$, forcing $q^k\\le q^{n-d+1}$, i.e. $k\\le n-d+1$.",
            "hint": "Puncture $d-1$ coordinates."
          },
          {
            "type": "multi-select",
            "q": "Which statements about the parameters $[n,k,d]$ of a $q$-ary block code are correct?",
            "choices": [
              "$d\\le n-k+1$ (Singleton).",
              "$|C|\\cdot V_q(n,\\lfloor (d-1)/2\\rfloor)\\le q^n$ (Hamming/sphere-packing).",
              "Every code attaining Singleton with equality is called perfect.",
              "Increasing $d$ at fixed $n,k$ is always achievable."
            ],
            "answer": [
              0,
              1
            ],
            "explain": "Singleton and the sphere-packing bound are both correct. 'MDS' (not 'perfect') describes Singleton-tight codes; 'perfect' means balls of radius $t$ tile the space (e.g. Hamming, Golay). And increasing $d$ at fixed $(n,k)$ generally requires giving up rate — there is no free distance.",
            "hint": "Two universal upper bounds vs. two interchanged definitions."
          }
        ]
      },
