              "Step 2: if a single bit at position $i$ is flipped, the syndrome $Hy^\\top$ equals the $i$-th column of $H$, i.e. the binary representation of $i$.",
              "Step 3: hence the decoder can identify which data bit got flipped, but parity bits (positions 1, 2, 4) are always correct — the channel cannot flip them.",
